WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
SPEAKER: If you run your
services on GKE or anywhere,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.670
really, you need to have a
way to get some basic signals

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.990
about service health.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.630
At a minimum, you want to
collect the golden signals

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.620
for your service.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.720
Traffic is a measure
of how much demand

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.780
the service is responding to,
usually measured in requests

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.400
or queries per second.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.640
Saturation is a measure of how
much of the available capacity

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.140
the service is using.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.090
Errors, or error
rate, should tell you

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.790
the fraction of requests
you're able to serve correctly

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.140
or incorrectly.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.405
And latency is a measure
of how quickly you

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.220
are able to serve your users.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.800
You can read more about golden
signals in the "Monitoring

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.990
Distributed Systems"
chapter of the SRE book.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.532
We'll link to that below.

00:00:38.532 --> 00:00:40.740
Of course, you need a way
to counter this information

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.970
for your services.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.427
My name is [? Uri. ?] I'm
an SRE at Google Cloud,

00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:47.010
and today, I'm going to show you
how to instrument your Python

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.220
services to emit metrics like
these with OpenTelemetry,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.410
an open source
observability library.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.720
Welcome back to
Engineering for Liability.

00:00:58.492 --> 00:01:00.200
We'll start with
walking through the code

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.673
to instrument your application.

00:01:01.673 --> 00:01:03.590
Then we'll have a look
at how to query and use

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.290
those metrics in
Cloud Monitoring.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.210
Our example service
is written in Python

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.620
using the Flask framework.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.900
It receives web requests
and does some processing

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.160
before responding.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.130
So we'd like to track the
request rate, error rate,

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.680
and the distribution of
the processing latency.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.690
As always, we'll link
to the full code sample,

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.080
but here's how you can add
metric instrumentation to it.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.930
First, we set up the
exporter to send metric data

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.490
to the OpenTelemetry collector.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.170
It's a dedicated
component we'll use

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.290
to collect telemetry and forward
it onto Cloud Monitoring,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.930
and we'll cover it in more
detail in our next episode.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.080
Next, we define the meter
provider, including information

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.240
about our service we'll be able
to use to query our metrics.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.420
Next, we define
our actual metrics.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.160
We'll use incrementing
counters to track

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.820
requests and errors and a
histogram to track latency.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.730
Finally, we use these
metrics to record our data.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.550
We increment the counters on
request in the error and record

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.410
latency before returning.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:00.200
And now, we have metrics to
represent the major signals

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.340
of application health--

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.030
request an error account
and a latency distribution.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.980
Now that our application
is instrumented,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.320
let's deploy it and have
a look at the metrics.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.030
Our application is
written in Python

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.091
and instrumented
with OpenTelemetry.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.080
We use this Docker file to
build a container image.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.240
We use this YAML file to deploy
our image to a GKE cluster.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.700
And here are the three replicas.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.980
Next, we create this
YAML file and use it

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.335
to deploy the
OpenTelemetry collector.

00:02:35.335 --> 00:02:36.960
And here it is running
in the same name

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.630
space as our application.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.630
Finally, we have some
pods generating traffic

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.220
against our service.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.170
Let's go to Metrics
Explorer and first look

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.520
for our total request metric.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.923
We'll group it by service ID.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:10.430
This shows the request
rate by [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.500
We can do the same for
the failed request metric.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.350
We can even have Cloud
Monitoring calculate

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.690
a ratio of the two for
us to easily measure

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.620
error rate for our application.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.375
Finally, let's also
graph request latency.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.460
Because this is a histogram
metric, we can use a heat map.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.890
This will automatically
calculate the average and tail

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.165
latencies for our service.

00:04:01.145 --> 00:04:03.270
So that's how you can deploy
a service instrumented

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.580
with OpenTelemetry to
GKE and view its health

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.100
metrics in Cloud Monitoring.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.688
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:09.688 --> 00:04:11.730
We learned how to use
OpenTelemetry to instrument

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.160
our service to admit basic
signals of application health,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.900
like traffic and
latency metrics,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.427
and how to query them
in Cloud Monitoring.

00:04:18.427 --> 00:04:20.760
I hope this helps you to keep
your applications reliable

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.079
and your users happy.

